ace="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Other Forms of DNA</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="390" name="Google Shape;390;p56"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="457200" y="838200"/><a:ext cx="8153400" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="On the left are the three forms of DNA helices. All have blue sugar backbones and yellow nitrogenous bases in the center. On the far left is the A form, whose complete helix measures 28 Angstroms (bracketed and labeled at left). The A form strands have closer associations with their sugar backbones, alternating with a large gap and a small one between helix rotations. Below is a view from the top: The backbone and nitrogenous bases look like a blue circle with a concentric yellow circle contained within it. To the right is the B form. It is a slightly less compressed version of DNA than the A form. From the top, shown below, the circular sugar backbone has a large yellow circle in the center, condensed tightly, with yellow nitrogenous bases fanning out rather like flower petals to meet with the inside of the sugar backbone circle. To the right of this is the Z form. Here the blue sugar backbones are irregular and wavy, winding loosely in the helix. The above view, shown below, depicts the sugar backbone and nitrogenous bases winding around one another in an almost ropelike fashion." id="391" name="Google Shape;391;p56"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip>